+2.1 Since $\eta_n\to0$, choose $N$ large enough that $p^{\otimes n}(T_n)=1-\eta_n>0$ and $\eta_n\le1/2$ for every $n\ge N$. For the finite prefix $n<N$, set $M_n=1$ and use the one-message code from step 1.2. For $n\ge N$, set $M_n=\lfloor e^{nR}\rfloor$ and apply the packing lemma [F5] to the conditional input law on $T_n$, with $d=e^{n(H(B|X)+\delta)}$ and $D^{-1}=(1-\eta_n)^{-1}e^{-n(S(\bar\rho_p)-\delta)}$. It gives expected average error at most $$2(u_n+2\sqrt{u_n})+4(M_n-1)(1-\eta_n)^{-1}e^{-n(\chi_p-2\delta)}.$$ Since $(1-\eta_n)^{-1}\le2$ for $n\ge N$ and $R<\chi_p-2\delta$, this tends to zero. The packing POVM has a failure effect $\Lambda_0$. Replace it by the $M_n$ effects $\Lambda_1\prime=\Lambda_1+\Lambda_0$ and $\Lambda_m\prime=\Lambda_m$ for $2\le m\le M_n$. They are positive and sum to $I$. For message 1 the success probability increases by the nonnegative quantity $\operatorname{tr}(\Lambda_0\rho_{x^n(1)})$, and for other messages it is unchanged; hence the merged average error is no larger for every codebook and obeys the same expectation bound. The codebook space is finite; order $\mathcal X$ once and choose its lexicographically first codebook whose average error is at most this expectation. This defines a deterministic code for each $n\ge N$ without any choice axiom, and the finite one-message prefix does not affect $n^{-1}\ln M_n\to R$. [F1, F4, F5, step 1.2, algebra, construct, choose]
 
 2.2 Let $p_i(x)=M^{-1}|\{m:x_i(m)=x\}|$ and $\bar\omega=M^{-1}\sum_m\omega_m$. By [F17], its $i$th marginal is $\bar\rho_i=\sum_xp_i(x)\rho_x$, which is a state by [F16]. For any bipartite state $\tau_{AC}$, tensor it with the unique state on a one-dimensional middle register $B=\mathbb C$: the four entropies in [F6] become $S(\tau_A),S(\tau_C),0,S(\tau_{AC})$, because tensoring with this factor leaves the spectrum unchanged. Thus [F6] implies $S(\tau_{AC})\le S(\tau_A)+S(\tau_C)$; iterating gives $S(\bar\omega)\le\sum_iS(\bar\rho_i)$. Product entropy is additive: diagonalize each $\rho_{x_i(m)}$ by [F12], so every positive product eigenvalue has logarithm equal to the sum of its coordinate logarithms by [F13]; zero eigenvalues contribute zero by [F2]. Summing these finite terms gives $S(\omega_m)=\sum_iS(\rho_{x_i(m)})$. Therefore $$\chi(\{1/M,\omega_m\})=S(\bar\omega)-\frac1M\sum_mS(\omega_m) \le\sum_{i=1}^n\left[S(\bar\rho_i)-\sum_xp_i(x)S(\rho_x)\right] =\sum_i\chi_{p_i}\le n\chi_*.$$ [F2, F6, F10, F11, F12, F13, F16, F17, step 1.1, algebra]
 
